  "id": "c281",
  "paper": "TECH1_LEGACY",
  "unit": "5",
  "unitTitle": "Database Management System",
  "sub": "Data and Database Administration",
  "def": "Data administration and database administration are the two organizational management functions responsible for governing data as a corporate asset (policy, standards, data quality, data dictionary) and for the technical operation of the DBMS itself (performance, security, backup/recovery), respectively.",
  "exp": "Data Administration (DA) is the higher-level, more managerial function: it sets data policies and standards, manages the data dictionary/repository and metadata, oversees data quality and data governance, coordinates data planning across the whole organization (often tied into enterprise data architecture and master data management), and resolves data ownership/stewardship questions between departments. Database Administration (DBA), by contrast, is the hands-on technical role: installing and configuring the DBMS, managing storage allocation and physical schema, tuning performance (indexing, query optimization, statistics), managing user accounts/roles/security privileges (GRANT/REVOKE), and executing backup and recovery procedures. In smaller organizations one person or team often performs both roles, but the exam distinguishes them by scope: DA is strategic/organization-wide and technology-independent, while DBA is tactical/technical and tied to a specific DBMS product. A data dictionary (or system catalog/metadata repository) - documenting every table, column, data type, constraint and relationship - is a shared artifact both roles depend on: DA uses it for governance and standards enforcement, DBA uses it as the DBMS's own internal metadata for query optimization and constraint enforcement. This subtopic operationalises the concerns raised in Managing Databases at an organizational-role level.",
  "facts": [
   "Data Administration (DA) = strategic, organization-wide policy, standards, data quality, governance",
   "Database Administration (DBA) = tactical, technical, DBMS-specific operations",
   "DBA responsibilities: installation, security/privileges, performance tuning, backup and recovery",
   "DA responsibilities: data policy, data dictionary/metadata oversight, data ownership/stewardship, planning",
   "A data dictionary (system catalog) stores metadata: table/column names, types, constraints, relationships",
   "In small organizations, DA and DBA functions may be combined into one role",
   "GRANT/REVOKE privilege management is a core DBA (not DA) technical activity"
  ],
  "traps": [
   "DA is policy/governance-focused (organization-wide, technology-independent); DBA is technical/DBMS-specific - swapping these is the main trap",
   "The data dictionary/system catalog is metadata ABOUT the data, not the data itself",
   "Backup/recovery and performance tuning are DBA tasks, not DA tasks",
   "Data ownership/stewardship disputes are resolved at the DA (organizational) level, not by the DBA",
   "Both roles may be held by the same person in a small shop, but the EXAM still expects the conceptual distinction"
  ],
  "mnem": "DA = Data Administration (policy); DBA = Database Administration (product/technical)",
  "rel": [
   "Managing Databases",
   "Managing Multiuser Databases",
   "The Database Environment",
   "Database Redesign"
  ]
