  "sub": "Rotation of solids and mental rotation",
  "def": "This question type asks the candidate to visualise how a 3-D solid (a cube, a labelled block, or an arrow/shape marker) appears after being rotated about a given axis, without physically manipulating it.",
  "exp": "The core technique is to fix a mental reference frame using the solid's UNCHANGING features first: identify an axis of rotation (which face/edge stays in the same general position) and rotate only the remaining features around that fixed axis, one step at a time, rather than trying to reposition the entire solid at once. For a cube rotated 90 degrees about a vertical axis, the top and bottom faces stay the same, while the four side faces cycle one position in the rotation's direction (front becomes left, left becomes back, back becomes right, right becomes front, for a clockwise-from-above rotation) - memorising this 'four side faces cycle, top/bottom fixed' rule for vertical-axis rotation (and the equivalent rule for rotation about a horizontal axis, where a different pair of opposite faces stays fixed) turns a hard 3-D visualisation into simple face-relabelling. For questions with an arrow or asymmetric marking on a solid, track that specific marking's new face and edge position after each incremental rotation step, applying the same fixed-axis-cycle logic, and always verify the final answer preserves the solid's actual face-adjacency relationships (a rotation never changes which faces are adjacent to which, only their apparent orientation).",
