  "sub": "Nets of solids",
  "def": "A net is a 2-D unfolded layout of a 3-D solid's faces that, when folded along the marked edges, reconstructs the solid; this question type asks which net folds into a given solid, or which solid a given net produces.",
  "exp": "The technique for cube nets is to use the 'opposite faces are two squares apart in a straight line' rule: in an unfolded cube net, two squares that are separated by exactly one intervening square along any straight row or column of the net (whether that row/column is part of the net's main strip or a branch) become OPPOSITE faces once folded - this rule lets a candidate identify opposite-face pairs directly from the flat net without physically folding it. There are 11 distinct valid nets for a cube (up to rotation/reflection); recognising common valid layouts (e.g., a cross/plus shape, a 'T' shape, a 3-3 staircase, a 2-2-2 staircase) versus invalid arrangements (e.g., a 2x3 block, which cannot fold into a cube without overlapping faces) speeds up 'which net is valid' questions. For non-cube solids (cuboids, pyramids, prisms), the technique is to mentally fold each face up along its marked edge one at a time, tracking which edges of adjacent faces must meet and coincide in length - a valid net's matching edges must be equal in length, and faces must not overlap when folded, which is the key check for identifying an invalid or mismatched net.",
